// =============================================================================
// load_graph_data.gsql — TigerGraph CSV Loading Jobs for FinancialGraph
// =============================================================================
//
// PURPOSE
//   Ingests the eight CSV files produced by build_graph_data.py into the
//   FinancialGraph schema installed by tigergraph_schema.gsql.  After these
//   jobs complete, the graph is queryable by the GraphRAG retrieval pipeline.
//
// PREREQUISITES
//   1. tigergraph_schema.gsql has been executed and the FinancialGraph schema
//      is installed and visible in TigerGraph Studio or via:
//        SHOW GRAPH FinancialGraph
//   2. CSV files exist at the configured paths (see PATH CONFIGURATION below).
//   3. TigerGraph Community Edition 4.2.2 or later.
//
// EXECUTION
//   Run the full file in one shot from the GSQL shell:
//     $ gsql load_graph_data.gsql
//
//   Or paste section-by-section into TigerGraph Studio's GSQL Editor.
//
// LOAD ORDER — vertices must exist before their edges are created
//   Phase 1 (Vertices):  Bank → Entity → Account → Transaction
//   Phase 2 (Edges):     TRANSFERRED_TO → BELONGS_TO_BANK
//                        → INITIATED_TRANSACTION → RECEIVED_TRANSACTION
//
//   Why this order matters:
//   TigerGraph validates that every edge endpoint vertex already exists at
//   load time.  If TRANSFERRED_TO edges are loaded before Account vertices,
//   the loader will reject every row whose FROM or TO account_id has not yet
//   been inserted.  The Bank-before-Account ordering satisfies the BELONGS_TO_BANK
//   constraint for the same reason.  Entities are loaded before Accounts because
//   ASSOCIATED_WITH edges (populated via a post-load query) point Account → Entity.
//
// RUNTIME PATH OVERRIDE
//   Every RUN LOADING JOB command below accepts a USING clause that overrides
//   the default file path without editing this file.  Example:
//
//     RUN LOADING JOB load_banks USING f_banks="/new/path/banks.csv"
//
//   This is the production-style configuration mechanism — set the paths in
//   RUN commands from your deployment script rather than hard-coding them here.
//
// SCALING CONSIDERATIONS
//   The HI-Small corpus generates ~5M Transaction vertices and ~20M+ edges.
//   Loading strategies for production scale:
//
//   1. Split large CSVs — TigerGraph parallelises across multiple files.
//      Split transactions.csv into 10 shards for 10x throughput:
//        DEFINE FILENAME f1 = "transactions_00.csv"
//        DEFINE FILENAME f2 = "transactions_01.csv"
//        LOAD f1 | f2 TO VERTEX Transaction VALUES (...)
//
//   2. Increase loader threads — set tigergraph.cfg loader.thread.count
//      to match available CPU cores before running heavy jobs.
//
//   3. Disable real-time stats during load — if STATS were enabled on edges,
//      they are updated asynchronously and do not block loading.
//
//   4. Upsert safety — all jobs use upsert semantics by default.
//      Re-running a job after a partial failure is safe; duplicate rows are
//      merged rather than inserted twice.
//
//   Estimated load time on a single-node 16 GB instance:
//     Vertices  (~5.4M total)  :  2–4 min
//     Edges     (~20M total)   :  8–15 min
//
// =============================================================================


USE GRAPH FinancialGraph


// =============================================================================
// PATH CONFIGURATION
// =============================================================================
//
// Set every DEFINE FILENAME path below to the absolute location of the CSV
// files on the TigerGraph server (or a shared mount accessible to the server).
//
// Common deployment scenarios:
//
//   Local / on-prem Linux:
//     /home/tigergraph/rag-benchmark-lab/datasets/graph/
//
//   Docker (volume-mounted):
//     /home/tigergraph/mydata/graph/
//
//   TigerGraph Cloud (files must be uploaded to the instance first):
//     /home/tigergraph/data/graph/
//
//   Windows development machine running TigerGraph in WSL2:
//     /mnt/c/study/projects/rag-benchmark-lab/datasets/graph/
//
// Change BASE_PATH in each DEFINE FILENAME — a simple find-and-replace of
// "/PATH/TO/datasets/graph" across this file is sufficient.


// =============================================================================
// PHASE 1 — VERTEX LOADING JOBS
// =============================================================================
//
// Load all vertex types before any edge types.  Within Phase 1, Bank and
// Entity vertices are loaded before Account because Account.home_bank is a
// denormalized reference, and ASSOCIATED_WITH (Account→Entity) must resolve
// at query time.  Transaction vertices are loaded last in Phase 1 because
// both INITIATED_TRANSACTION (Account→Transaction) and RECEIVED_TRANSACTION
// (Transaction→Account) reference them; loading after Account ensures all
// endpoint vertex types are present when edge jobs run in Phase 2.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 1: Bank vertices  —  banks.csv
//
// CSV columns:  bank_id
//
// bank_name and country are not in the generated CSV (build_graph_data.py
// only captures bank_id from transaction metadata).  Those attributes default
// to "" and can be enriched post-load from HI-Small_accounts.csv using the
// GSQL update query shown in the validation section below.
//
// Why Bank first:  Account.home_bank is a STRING reference to a Bank vertex.
// Loading Bank before Account means any query that traverses BELONGS_TO_BANK
// immediately after the load will resolve correctly without waiting for a
// deferred vertex insertion.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_banks FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_banks = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/banks.csv";

    // VALUES position order must match CREATE VERTEX attribute order:
    //   (PRIMARY_ID: bank_id,  bank_name,  country)
    LOAD f_banks
        TO VERTEX Bank VALUES (
            $"bank_id",     // PRIMARY_ID → bank_id
            _,              // bank_name  → default ""
            _               // country    → default ""
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 2: Entity vertices  —  entities.csv
//
// CSV columns:  entity_id,  entity_name,  representative_account
//
// entity_type is not captured in build_graph_data.py (it can be derived from
// entity_name, e.g. "Corporation #..." → "Corporation").  Set to "" for now;
// a post-load UPDATE query can parse and backfill it without reloading.
//
// Why Entity before Account:  ASSOCIATED_WITH edges point Account → Entity.
// Having Entity vertices present before Account load does not affect the edge
// load (which runs in Phase 2), but ensures consistent graph state throughout.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_entities FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_entities = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/entities.csv";

    // CREATE VERTEX Entity attribute order:
    //   (PRIMARY_ID: entity_id,  entity_name,  entity_type,  representative_account)
    LOAD f_entities
        TO VERTEX Entity VALUES (
            $"entity_id",              // PRIMARY_ID → entity_id
            $"entity_name",            // entity_name
            _,                         // entity_type → default "" (enrich post-load)
            $"representative_account"  // representative_account
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 3: Account vertices  —  accounts.csv
//
// CSV columns:  account_id,  home_bank,  entity_name
//
// account_type is not present in the generated CSV.  It defaults to "unknown".
// Enrich post-load from a business rules query (e.g. accounts with >1000
// outgoing transfers → "high_volume"; accounts with entity_type "Corporation"
// → "corporate") without reloading the full CSV.
//
// Note: CSV column order differs from schema attribute order.
//   CSV:    account_id | home_bank | entity_name
//   Schema: account_id | entity_name | account_type | home_bank
// The $"column_name" reference syntax handles this — order in VALUES follows
// schema definition order, but column names are resolved from the CSV header.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_accounts FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_accounts = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/accounts.csv";

    // CREATE VERTEX Account attribute order:
    //   (PRIMARY_ID: account_id,  entity_name,  account_type,  home_bank)
    LOAD f_accounts
        TO VERTEX Account VALUES (
            $"account_id",   // PRIMARY_ID → account_id
            $"entity_name",  // entity_name
            _,               // account_type → default "unknown"
            $"home_bank"     // home_bank
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 4: Transaction vertices  —  transactions.csv
//
// CSV columns:
//   transaction_id, amount_paid, payment_currency, amount_received,
//   receiving_currency, payment_format, timestamp, is_laundering,
//   is_self_transfer, is_cross_bank, is_cross_currency, transfer_type
//
// ATTRIBUTE REMAPPING (CSV column → schema attribute):
//   payment_format  → payment_type      (schema normalises to generic "type")
//   is_laundering   → laundering_flag   (schema uses flag naming convention)
//
// laundering_flag is the ground-truth AML label from the HI-Small synthetic
// injection.  Preserving it in the graph lets traversal queries filter to
// confirmed-laundering paths during benchmark evaluation without a JOIN.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_transactions FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_transactions = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/transactions.csv";

    // CREATE VERTEX Transaction attribute order:
    //   (PRIMARY_ID: transaction_id,
    //    amount_paid, payment_currency,
    //    amount_received, receiving_currency,
    //    payment_type,     ← from payment_format in CSV
    //    timestamp,
    //    laundering_flag,  ← from is_laundering in CSV
    //    is_self_transfer, is_cross_bank, is_cross_currency,
    //    transfer_type)
    LOAD f_transactions
        TO VERTEX Transaction VALUES (
            $"transaction_id",     // PRIMARY_ID → transaction_id
            $"amount_paid",        // amount_paid     (DOUBLE)
            $"payment_currency",   // payment_currency
            $"amount_received",    // amount_received (DOUBLE)
            $"receiving_currency", // receiving_currency
            $"payment_format",     // payment_type    ← remapped from payment_format
            $"timestamp",          // timestamp
            $"is_laundering",      // laundering_flag ← remapped from is_laundering
            $"is_self_transfer",   // is_self_transfer (INT 0/1)
            $"is_cross_bank",      // is_cross_bank    (INT 0/1)
            $"is_cross_currency",  // is_cross_currency (INT 0/1)
            $"transfer_type"       // transfer_type (SELF_TRANSFER | SAME_BANK | CROSS_BANK)
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// =============================================================================
// PHASE 2 — EDGE LOADING JOBS
// =============================================================================
//
// Edge loads run after all vertex jobs complete.  TigerGraph's loader checks
// that every FROM and TO vertex ID already exists in the graph; rows whose
// endpoints are missing are silently dropped (not errors).  This means:
//
//   • Running edge jobs before vertex jobs → all rows dropped, zero edges loaded
//   • Partial vertex load followed by edge load → subset of edges loaded
//
// For the full 5M corpus, verify Phase 1 vertex counts before starting Phase 2.
// Use the validation queries at the end of this file to confirm.
//
// Edge load order within Phase 2 is flexible — there are no inter-edge
// dependencies.  The order below matches data volume (largest jobs first) so
// that the most expensive work starts while smaller jobs are queued.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 5: TRANSFERRED_TO edges  —  transferred_edges.csv
//
// CSV columns:
//   from_account, to_account, transaction_id, amount_paid,
//   payment_currency, timestamp, transfer_type, is_laundering
//
// This is the primary traversal edge for GraphRAG retrieval.
// At full corpus scale (~5M rows) this is the largest single edge file.
// Consider splitting into shards for parallel loading in production.
//
// transfer_type on the edge (SELF_TRANSFER | SAME_BANK | CROSS_BANK) allows
// traversal predicates to filter path types without fetching Transaction
// vertices, reducing per-hop I/O in multi-hop cycle detection queries.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_transferred_to_edges FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_transferred = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/transferred_edges.csv";

    // Edge VALUES order: (FROM_id, TO_id, attr1, attr2, ...)
    // Attribute order follows CREATE DIRECTED EDGE TRANSFERRED_TO definition:
    //   transaction_id, amount_paid, payment_currency, timestamp,
    //   transfer_type, is_laundering
    LOAD f_transferred
        TO EDGE TRANSFERRED_TO VALUES (
            $"from_account",     // FROM Account (sender)
            $"to_account",       // TO   Account (receiver)
            $"transaction_id",   // transaction_id
            $"amount_paid",      // amount_paid  (DOUBLE)
            $"payment_currency", // payment_currency
            $"timestamp",        // timestamp
            $"transfer_type",    // transfer_type
            $"is_laundering"     // is_laundering (INT 0/1)
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 6: BELONGS_TO_BANK edges  —  bank_edges.csv
//
// CSV columns:  account_id,  bank_id
//
// One row per unique account — total edges equals total Account vertices.
// This edge is the institutional clustering link used by queries that group
// accounts by bank and compute cross-institution flow ratios.
// No edge attributes; the FROM/TO IDs carry all necessary information.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_belongs_to_bank_edges FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_bank_edges = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/bank_edges.csv";

    LOAD f_bank_edges
        TO EDGE BELONGS_TO_BANK VALUES (
            $"account_id",  // FROM Account
            $"bank_id"      // TO   Bank
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 7: INITIATED_TRANSACTION edges  —  initiated_edges.csv
//
// CSV columns:  account_id,  transaction_id,  timestamp
//
// Sender-to-event link: FROM Account TO Transaction.
// CSV column order (account_id, transaction_id) matches edge direction
// (FROM Account, TO Transaction), so no column reordering is needed.
//
// GraphRAG use: "find all transactions initiated by account X in window W"
// resolves in one hop — no full TRANSFERRED_TO scan required.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_initiated_transaction_edges FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_initiated = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/initiated_edges.csv";

    // Edge attribute order: (FROM_id, TO_id, timestamp)
    LOAD f_initiated
        TO EDGE INITIATED_TRANSACTION VALUES (
            $"account_id",     // FROM Account (sender)
            $"transaction_id", // TO   Transaction
            $"timestamp"       // timestamp
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JOB 8: RECEIVED_TRANSACTION edges  —  received_edges.csv
//
// CSV columns:  account_id,  transaction_id,  timestamp
//
// IMPORTANT — column order is REVERSED relative to edge direction.
// The edge is defined as:  FROM Transaction  TO Account
// But the CSV stores:       account_id (receiver)  |  transaction_id
//
// Therefore VALUES must supply ($"transaction_id", $"account_id", ...) —
// FROM Transaction first, then TO Account — even though transaction_id is
// the second CSV column.  The $"column_name" reference syntax handles this
// without requiring a reordered CSV file.
//
// Why this edge direction?  It makes multi-hop chain queries natural:
//   Account -[INITIATED_TRANSACTION]-> Transaction -[RECEIVED_TRANSACTION]-> Account
// reads left-to-right as money flowing through the event vertex to the
// recipient, matching analyst intuition about fund flow.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE LOADING JOB load_received_transaction_edges FOR GRAPH FinancialGraph {
    DEFINE FILENAME f_received = "/home/tigergraph/data/graph/received_edges.csv";

    // FROM = Transaction, TO = Account  (reversed from CSV column order)
    LOAD f_received
        TO EDGE RECEIVED_TRANSACTION VALUES (
            $"transaction_id", // FROM Transaction  ← second CSV column
            $"account_id",     // TO   Account      ← first CSV column
            $"timestamp"       // timestamp
        )
        USING SEPARATOR = ",", HEADER = "true";
}


// =============================================================================
// RUN LOADING JOBS — PHASE 1: VERTICES
// =============================================================================
//
// Execute in this exact order.  Each RUN command blocks until the job
// completes or fails before the next one starts.
//
// To override the file path at runtime without editing this file:
//   RUN LOADING JOB load_banks USING f_banks="/alternative/path/banks.csv"
//
// To monitor a running job from another GSQL session:
//   SHOW LOADING STATUS load_banks
//
// To abort a job in progress:
//   ABORT LOADING JOB load_banks

RUN LOADING JOB load_banks
RUN LOADING JOB load_entities
RUN LOADING JOB load_accounts
RUN LOADING JOB load_transactions


// =============================================================================
// RUN LOADING JOBS — PHASE 2: EDGES
// =============================================================================
//
// Do not run Phase 2 until Phase 1 is confirmed complete.
// Use the validation queries below to verify vertex counts before proceeding.
//
// Edge load order within Phase 2 is independent; jobs can be run in any
// sequence.  The order below processes the largest file first (TRANSFERRED_TO)
// so peak I/O happens while the system is freshest.

RUN LOADING JOB load_transferred_to_edges
RUN LOADING JOB load_belongs_to_bank_edges
RUN LOADING JOB load_initiated_transaction_edges
RUN LOADING JOB load_received_transaction_edges


// =============================================================================
// VALIDATION QUERIES
// =============================================================================
//
// WHY VALIDATION MATTERS FOR GRAPHRAG RETRIEVAL
//
// A graph traversal retrieval pipeline fails silently if the graph is
// partially loaded.  Unlike vector search (where a missing chunk means a
// slightly worse result), a missing vertex in a graph means an edge endpoint
// is dangling — traversal terminates early and returns an incomplete subgraph.
// The LLM then reasons from incomplete evidence, producing answers that appear
// confident but miss entire transaction chains.
//
// Validate in two phases:
//   Phase 1 check  — vertex counts match expected totals from build_graph_data.py
//   Phase 2 check  — edge counts match; TRANSFERRED_TO == Transaction count
//
// Expected counts for the FULL 5M corpus (dev mode values are proportional):
//   Account         ~300,000 – 400,000  unique accounts
//   Bank            ~200               unique bank codes
//   Entity          ~20,000            unique legal entities (from accounts CSV)
//   Transaction     ~5,078,345         one per narrative row
//   TRANSFERRED_TO  ~5,078,345         one per transaction (Account→Account)
//   BELONGS_TO_BANK ~300,000 – 400,000 one per Account vertex
//   INITIATED       ~5,078,345         one per transaction (sender link)
//   RECEIVED        ~5,078,345         one per transaction (receiver link)
//
// For the DEV subset (first 100,000 rows from build_graph_data.py --dev):
//   Account         ~81,352
//   Bank            ~4,598
//   Entity          ~57,285
//   Transaction     100,000
//   TRANSFERRED_TO  100,000
//   BELONGS_TO_BANK ~81,352
//   INITIATED       100,000
//   RECEIVED        100,000
//
// Run these SELECT statements in the GSQL shell after loading completes.
// They execute as interpreted queries — no INSTALL QUERY step required.

// --- Vertex counts ----------------------------------------------------------

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Account

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Bank

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Entity

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Transaction

// --- Edge counts ------------------------------------------------------------

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Account -(TRANSFERRED_TO)-> Account

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Account -(BELONGS_TO_BANK)-> Bank

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Account -(INITIATED_TRANSACTION)-> Transaction

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Transaction -(RECEIVED_TRANSACTION)-> Account

// --- Consistency check: laundering label distribution ----------------------
//
// Confirms that laundering_flag values were loaded correctly.
// In the HI-Small dataset roughly 2–5% of transactions are synthetic AML
// injections (laundering_flag = 1).  A count of 0 for flag=1 means the
// is_laundering → laundering_flag remapping failed during transaction load.

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Transaction WHERE laundering_flag == 1

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Transaction WHERE laundering_flag == 0

// --- Connectivity spot-check: every Account should belong to exactly one Bank
//
// If BELONGS_TO_BANK edge count significantly differs from Account count,
// some accounts were loaded without a home bank — check bank_edges.csv for
// missing rows or mismatched bank_id values.

// --- Post-load enrichment hooks (run manually as needed) --------------------
//
